// The real "@par_call" -- called by the compiler's own "par foo(...)"
// desugaring (see caspien-compiler's own CLAUDE.md, "par"/"await"
// real-thread desugaring) with the trampoline's own address (`fn`) and
// the freshly-constructed, already-`gt_register`ed handle pointer
// (`handle`, erased to a bare "raw mut u8" -- the fixed shape every
// "@par_call"/"@await_call" must have, since the concrete
// "AsyncHandle_<T>"/hidden-handle struct varies per "@async" function
// and this must be one single, fixed-signature function for the whole
// compilation unit).
//
// "par" is fire-and-forget: it starts the real OS thread and returns
// immediately, handing the caller back the handle itself (still
// pending) to poll/`:resolve()` later -- so this body is nothing more
// than a bare "pthread_create". Unlike "@await_call" just below, it
// never reads the pthread_t `pthread_create` writes out (nothing here
// ever joins the thread it started) -- "threadId" on the handle struct
// is kept purely as general-purpose plumbing for whatever a future
// pass adds here (the language's own "join" keyword/"JOIN" bytecode
// mnemonic were removed entirely, not deferred -- there is currently
// no user-facing way to join a "par"'d thread at all), so a throwaway
// local is used here instead of writing through the handle's own
// (structurally unknown at this fixed, erased signature) "value"/
// "threadId" field offsets.
import "libc.caspien"

@par_call
@pub
func par_call(fn: raw imut u8, handle: raw mut u8) void{
	let tid = mut 0
	unsafe extern raw{
		pthread_create(raw tid, null, fn, handle)
	}
}
